{"id": 7, "ts": 1780580116.654102, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I appreciate Sarah\u2019s focus on Candidate\u202fA\u2019s strong technical profile\u2014anticipating dangerous situations, seeing complex connections, excellent spatial vision, and very good leadership are indeed critical for long\u2011distance operations. However, the additional facts I have on A\u2014being regarded as a show\u2011off and not open to new ideas\u2014raise legitimate concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability, especially on extended international flights where feedback and flexibility are essential.\nWhen we look at the alternatives, Candidate\u202fB is very conscientious and handles stress well, but his tendency to make nasty remarks about colleagues directly threatens professional communication and crew harmony. Candidate\u202fC keeps calm in a crisis and understands complicated technology, yet his difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric reputation, and lack of willingness to further his education undermine crew cooperation and adaptability. Candidate\u202fD solves problems extremely well, concentrates well, and takes responsibility seriously, but his hot temper and know\u2011it\u2011all attitude could erode a positive cockpit environment.\nGiven the current evidence, A\u2019s technical competence and leadership still outweigh the noted interpersonal drawbacks, provided we can address his openness to feedback during onboarding. Therefore, I continue to support Candidate\u202fA as the provisional recommendation, while urging us to probe further into each candidate\u2019s stress resilience and crew\u2011interaction behaviors before finalizing."}
